","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":84,"newStart":1,"newLines":54,"lines":[" {"proposals": [","   {","-    "id": "rule30_alternating_step",","-    "lean_name": "rule30_alternating_step",","-    "statement": "theorem rule30_alternating_step (X : Config) (L : ℕ)\n    (h : ∀ j < L + 2, X (-(j : ℤ)) = decide (j % 2 = 0)) :\n    ∀ j < L + 1, rule30 X (-(j : ℤ)) = decide (j % 2 = 0) := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "The alternating row `(10)^∞` is one of rule 30's three fixed points (Wolfram 1986 Table 6.2, crystals item 22). This is that fact in the only form the rest of the tier can use: a FINITE alternating stretch running left from the origin survives one step, one cell shorter at its left end, and -- this is the part that is not just 'it is a fixed point' -- the cell AT the origin survives regardless of what sits to the right of it, because the origin is black there and the `or` in `left XOR (centre OR right)` swallows the right neighbour. So a stretch that is alternating on positions `-(L+1)..0` is alternating on `-L..0` one step later, whatever the right half-line does. Each cell is one `rule30_eq` and a Bool case split; the only friction is that the three neighbours of `-(j)` are `-(j+1)`, `-(j)`, `-(j-1)`, so the index arithmetic crosses a ℕ-to-ℤ cast.",","-    "disclaims": "Says nothing about the centre column on its own, and nothing about any prize. It is the engine of column_black_run_of_alternating and column_alternating_shrink and has no other purpose.",","-    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","+    "id": "centerColumn_black_run_lt_start",","+    "lean_name": "centerColumn_black_run_lt_start",","+    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_black_run_lt_start (a L : ℕ) (ha : 1 ≤ a)\n    (h : ∀ s ≤ L, centerColumn (a + s) = true) : L < a := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "While the centre column stays black, the cells immediately left of it at the run's own starting row are forced to alternate black-white-black, one cell deeper per further black step — that is `column_alternating_of_black_run`, already closed, read at the single-seed picture. The cone will not allow a deep checkerboard: at row `a` the two leftmost cells that exist at all, at `-a` and `-(a-1)`, are both black (`evolve_left_edge` and `evolve_left_second_diagonal`), while alternation says cells at consecutive depths are opposite. So the forced block cannot reach depth `a`, and therefore the run cannot last `a` further steps. `1 ≤ a` is needed and is not a technicality: the seed's own opening run at `a = 0` is two cells long, and there the cone's left edge and the origin are the same cell. Measured: 0 violations over 1,000,344 maximal runs to N = 2,000,000, and exactly tight at `a = 3`, where the run `111` at times 3, 4, 5 is the first non-trivial run there is (`explorer/p2seed_runs.mjs`). Orientation-sensitive: Talus measured that rule 86, rule 30 mirrored, has the same centre-column runs and fails the underlying alternation at 316 of 968 cells, so this is not a symmetric check.",","+    "disclaims": "Nothing towards Prize 2 on its own. It bounds one run and says nothing about how often runs occur or about any density. Its only ingredient is the cone, and by Talus's fence (docs/attacks/2026-09-10-prize-2-s-residual-…, C2) the cone's sole consequence for the centre column is a run bound — every other local law rule 30 supplies is quantified over `Config` and so holds of every `Bool` sequence.",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "  have alt_succ : ∀ j : ℕ, (decide ((j + 1) % 2 = 0)) = !(decide (j % 2 = 0)) := by\n    intro j\n    rcases Nat.mod_two_eq_zero_or_one j with hp | hp <;> simp [Nat.add_mod, hp]\n  intro j hj\n  rw [rule30_eq]\n  have hj0 : X (-(j : ℤ)) = decide (j % 2 = 0) := h j (by omega)\n  have hjm : X (-(j : ℤ) - 1) = !(decide (j % 2 = 0)) := by\n    have hx := h (j + 1) (by omega)\n    rw [alt_succ] at hx\n    rw [← hx]\n    congr 1\n    push_cast\n    ring\n  have hor : (X (-(j : ℤ)) || X (-(j : ℤ) + 1)) = true := by\n    cases j with\n    | zero => rw [hj0]; simp\n    | succ n =>\n        have hx := h n (by omega)\n        have he : X (-((n : ℕ) + 1 : ℤ) + 1) = decide (n % 2 = 0) := by\n          rw [← hx]; congr 1; push_cast; ring\n        push_cast at hj0 he ⊢\n        rw [hj0, he, alt_succ]\n        cases (decide (n % 2 = 0)) <;> simp\n  rw [hjm, hor]\n  cases (decide (j % 2 = 0)) <;> simp",","-      "imports": []","-    }","-  },","-  {","-    "id": "column_black_run_of_alternating",","-    "lean_name": "column_black_run_of_alternating",","-    "statement": "theorem column_black_run_of_alternating (X : Config) (t L : ℕ)\n    (h : ∀ j < L + 1, column X (-(j : ℤ)) t = decide (j % 2 = 0))\n    (s : ℕ) (hs : s ≤ L) : column X 0 (t + s) = true := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "An alternating stretch of depth `L + 1` left of the origin holds the centre cell black for the next `L + 1` steps. Induction on `s`, one application of rule30_alternating_step per step: the stretch shortens by one cell a step and stays alternating, so the origin keeps reading a black cell under it, and the stretch is long enough to last `L` more steps. The direction that matters: this bounds the centre column from BELOW using only the left half of the row, so nothing about column 1 or the right half-line enters -- which is the asymmetry obstruction 9 says the black side of the centre column has and the white side does not.",","-    "disclaims": "Not a prize node on its own. It is one half of the correspondence 'black run of the centre column = depth of the alternating background'; the converse is column_alternating_of_black_run.",","-    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","-    "route": {","-      "tactics": "  induction s generalizing t L with\n  | zero =>\n      have := h 0 (by omega)\n      simpa using this\n  | succ n ih =>\n      cases L with\n      | zero => omega\n      | succ M =>\n          have hstep : ∀ j < M + 1, column X (-(j : ℤ)) (t + 1) = decide (j % 2 = 0) := by\n            have hs2 := rule30_alternating_step (evolveFrom X t) M (by\n              intro j hjj\n              have := h j (by omega)\n              simpa [column] using this)\n            intro j hjj\n            have hx := hs2 j hjj\n            simpa [column, evolveFrom_succ] using hx\n          have := ih (t := t + 1) (L := M) hstep (by omega)\n          simpa [Nat.add_assoc, Nat.add_comm, Nat.add_left_comm] using this",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.Rule30AlternatingStep"]","+      "tactics": "  by_contra hc\n  have hc : a ≤ L := by omega\n  have hcol : ∀ s ≤ L, column initialConfig 0 (a + s) = true := h\n  have hedge := column_alternating_of_black_run initialConfig a L hcol a hc\n  have hsec := column_alternating_of_black_run initialConfig a L hcol (a - 1) (by omega)\n  rw [show column initialConfig (-(a : ℤ)) a = evolve a (-(a : ℤ)) from rfl,\n    evolve_left_edge a] at hedge\n  have h2 : evolve a (-((a - 1 : ℕ) : ℤ)) = true := by\n    have hh := evolve_left_second_diagonal (a - 1)\n    rwa [show a - 1 + 1 = a from by omega] at hh\n  rw [show column initialConfig (-((a - 1 : ℕ) : ℤ)) a = evolve a (-((a - 1 : ℕ) : ℤ)) from rfl,\n    h2] at hsec\n  simp at hedge hsec\n  omega",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.ColumnAlternatingOfBlackRun", "Rule30.Proofs.EvolveLeftEdge", "Rule30.Proofs.EvolveLeftSecondDiagonal"]","     },","     "witness": {","-      "expression": "(List.range 7).all (fun t => (List.range 5).all (fun j => ((List.range (j + 1)).all (fun s => evolve (t + s) 0) == (List.range (j + 1)).all (fun i => evolve t (-(i : ℤ)) == decide (i % 2 = 0)))))",","+      "expression": "(List.range 9).all fun a => (List.range 9).all fun L =>\n  decide (a < 1) || decide (L < a) ||\n    !((List.range (L + 1)).all fun s => centerColumn (a + s))",","       "imports": [],","-      "range": "t < 7, j < 5 -- the full iff on the seed, both directions at once"","+      "range": "a ≤ 8 and L ≤ 8. The cheap disjuncts come first so the automaton is evaluated only where the claim has content, at 1 ≤ a ≤ L, and the inner `all` stops at the first white cell — so no read goes past depth 12."","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "column_alternating_of_black_run",","-    "lean_name": "column_alternating_of_black_run",","-    "statement": "theorem column_alternating_of_black_run (X : Config) (t L : ℕ)\n    (h : ∀ s ≤ L, column X 0 (t + s) = true)\n    (j : ℕ) (hj : j ≤ L) : column X (-(j : ℤ)) t = decide (j % 2 = 0) := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "The converse: `L + 1` consecutive black centre cells FORCE the row to be alternating `1 0 1 0 ...` for `L + 1` cells left of the origin. Why: a black centre pins the next centre cell to the negation of the cell at `-1` (column_succ_of_black), so a black run makes the cell at `-1` white; then rule 30 read at `-1`, with a white cell there and a black one at `0`, is a plain negation and hands back the cell at `-2`; and so on inward, one cell per diagonal. Together with column_black_run_of_alternating this is an exact equality, not two inequalities: the length of the centre column's black run at time `t` IS the depth to which the alternating fixed point reaches left of the origin. Verified with zero mismatches over 3,960 rows of the seed and six random rows in explorer/altrun.mjs. The work is that the induction is strong: solving for the cell at `-(j+1)` needs the cell at `-(j-1)` as well as at `-(j)`, so it recurses two steps back, not one.",","-    "disclaims": "Not a prize node on its own; the pair with column_black_run_of_alternating is the vocabulary the two P1 nodes below are stated in. As mathematics this is very small -- it is two lines from Wolfram 1986's classification of rule 30's fixed points, which is already crystal 22 -- and I found no statement of it in the held sources, so call it project-internal shading into known, not novel.",","-    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","-    "route": {","-      "tactics": "  have alt_succ : ∀ j : ℕ, (decide ((j + 1) % 2 = 0)) = !(decide (j % 2 = 0)) := by\n    intro j\n    rcases Nat.mod_two_eq_zero_or_one j with hp | hp <;> simp [Nat.add_mod, hp]\n  have aux : ∀ j : ℕ, ∀ (X : Config) (t L : ℕ),\n      (∀ s ≤ L, column X 0 (t + s) = true) → j ≤ L →\n      column X (-(j : ℤ)) t = decide (j % 2 = 0) := by\n    intro j\n    induction j using Nat.strong_induction_on with\n    | _ j ih =>\n      match j, ih with\n      | 0, _ => intro X t L h _; simpa using h 0 (by omega)\n      | (n + 1), ih =>\n        intro X t L h hj\n        have hn : column X (-(n : ℤ)) t = decide (n % 2 = 0) :=\n          ih n (by omega) X t L h (by omega)\n        have hor : (column X (-(n : ℤ)) t || column X (-(n : ℤ) + 1) t) = true := by\n          cases n with\n          | zero => rw [hn]; simp\n          | succ m =>\n              have hm : column X (-(m : ℤ)) t = decide (m % 2 = 0) :=\n                ih m (by omega) X t L h (by omega)\n              have he : column X (-((m : ℕ) + 1 : ℤ) + 1) t = decide (m % 2 = 0) := by\n                rw [← hm]; congr 1; push_cast; ring\n              push_cast at hn he ⊢\n              rw [hn, he, alt_succ]\n              cases (decide (m % 2 = 0)) <;> simp\n        have hnext : column X (-(n : ℤ)) (t + 1) = decide (n % 2 = 0) := by\n          cases L with\n          | zero => omega\n          | succ M =>\n              refine ih n (by omega) X (t + 1) M ?_ (by omega)\n              intro s hs\n              have := h (s + 1) (by omega)\n              simpa [Nat.add_assoc, Nat.add_comm, Nat.add_left_comm] using this\n        have hstep : column X (-(n : ℤ)) (t + 1)\n            = xor (column X (-(n : ℤ) - 1) t)\n                (column X (-(n : ℤ)) t || column X (-(n : ℤ) + 1) t) := by\n          simp [column, evolveFrom_succ, rule30_eq]\n        rw [hnext, hor] at hstep\n        have hval : column X (-(n : ℤ) - 1) t = !(decide (n % 2 = 0)) := by\n          cases hb : column X (-(n : ℤ) - 1) t <;> rw [hb] at hstep <;>\n            simp at hstep <;> simp [hstep]\n        have hfin : column X (-((n : ℕ) + 1 : ℤ)) t = !(decide (n % 2 = 0)) := by\n          rw [← hval]; congr 1; push_cast; ring\n        push_cast\n        rw [hfin, alt_succ]\n  exact aux j X t L h hj",","-      "imports": []","-    },","+    "id": "centerColumn_white_run_lt_start",","+    "lean_name": "centerColumn_white_run_lt_start",","+    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_white_run_lt_start (a L : ℕ) (ha : 1 ≤ a)\n    (h : ∀ s ≤ L, centerColumn (a + s) = false) : L < 3 * a := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "The white twin of the black-run bound, and it splits on the colour of the column one cell left of the origin. That column is monotone inside a white run — once black it stays black, which is `white_run_forbidden`, closed — so let `t₀` be the row of the run where it turns black (or the row after the run, if it never does). Before `t₀` the centre and column `-1` are both white, and reading rule 30 backwards (`sideways_inverse` at `i = -j`) makes the next cell left white too, one deeper per row: an all-white triangle at row `a` of depth `t₀ - a`, which the black left edge at `-a` stops, giving `t₀ < 2 * a`. From `t₀` on, column `-1` is black under a white centre, and the same backwards reading forces the checkerboard with the *opposite* phase to a black run's — white at even depth, black at odd — to depth `a + L - t₀` at row `t₀`, which the cone's two adjacent black cells at `-t₀` and `-(t₀-1)` stop, giving `a + L < 2 * t₀ + 2`. Substituting the first into the second gives `L < 3 * a`. Measured: both forced patterns hold with 0 failures over 7,521 maximal white runs to row 30,000, and `t₀ / a` never exceeds 1.167 (`explorer/p2seed_white.mjs`). The conclusion is far from tight — over 2,000,000 rows every maximal white run beginning at `a ≥ 1` satisfies `length ≤ a`, worst ratio 0.5 (`explorer/p2seed_runs.mjs`) — and `3` is stated because `3` is what this argument gives.",","+    "disclaims": "Not the sharp bound, and nothing here explains the gap: measured, a maximal white run beginning at `a` has length at most `a/2`, where this proves only `< 3a`. Nothing towards Prize 2 on its own.",","     "witness": {","-      "expression": "(List.range 7).all (fun t => (List.range 5).all (fun j => ((List.range (j + 1)).all (fun s => evolve (t + s) 0) == (List.range (j + 1)).all (fun i => evolve t (-(i : ℤ)) == decide (i % 2 = 0)))))",","+      "expression": "(List.range 6).all fun a => (List.range 16).all fun L =>\n  decide (a < 1) || decide (L < 3 * a) ||\n    !((List.range (L + 1)).all fun s => !centerColumn (a + s))",","       "imports": [],","-      "range": "t < 7, j < 5 -- the full iff on the seed, both directions at once"","+      "range": "a ≤ 5 and L ≤ 15. Weak on purpose: with the cheap disjuncts first, the automaton is read only where `L ≥ 3a`, and the white stretch there breaks within a cell or two, so nothing deep is checked. The bound's content is at larger `a` and is covered by the scripts named in the reason, not by this."","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "column_alternating_shrink",","-    "lean_name": "column_alternating_shrink",","-    "statement": "theorem column_alternating_shrink (X : Config) (t L : ℕ)\n    (h : ∀ j < L + 1, column X (-(j : ℤ)) t = decide (j % 2 = 0))\n    (hmax : column X (-((L : ℕ) + 1 : ℤ)) t ≠ decide ((L + 1) % 2 = 0)) :\n    (∀ j < L, column X (-(j : ℤ)) (t + 1) = decide (j % 2 = 0))\n      ∧ column X (-(L : ℤ)) (t + 1) ≠ decide (L % 2 = 0) := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "The alternating region left of the origin is a COUNTDOWN: if its depth is exactly `L + 1` at time `t` then it is exactly `L` at time `t + 1` -- it cannot grow, cannot hold, and cannot shrink by two. The `≥` half is rule30_alternating_step; the sharp half is one line, because the cell that would extend the region is the negation of the first cell that broke the pattern, and negating a broken pattern breaks it again. Why this is the node the tier exists to reach: with column_black_run_of_alternating and column_alternating_of_black_run it says the centre column's black runs cannot be built up gradually. Each black run is CREATED in a single step, and it can only be created at a time when the centre cell is white -- because that is the only time the countdown is not already running. So the whole black side of the centre column is decided at its white times, which is exactly where obstruction 9 says the P1 residual lives; the two arrive at the same seam from opposite directions.",","-    "disclaims": "Proves no prize. It constrains where the hypothesis of centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_deep_alternating could possibly be established, and does not establish it.",","-    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","-    "route": {","-      "tactics": "  have alt_succ : ∀ j : ℕ, (decide ((j + 1) % 2 = 0)) = !(decide (j % 2 = 0)) := by\n    intro j\n    rcases Nat.mod_two_eq_zero_or_one j with hp | hp <;> simp [Nat.add_mod, hp]\n  constructor\n  · intro j hj\n    cases L with\n    | zero => omega\n    | succ M =>\n        have hs2 := rule30_alternating_step (evolveFrom X t) M (by\n          intro i hii\n          have := h i (by omega)\n          simpa [column] using this)\n        have hx := hs2 j (by omega)\n        simpa [column, evolveFrom_succ] using hx\n  · have hstep : column X (-(L : ℤ)) (t + 1)\n        = xor (column X (-(L : ℤ) - 1) t)\n            (column X (-(L : ℤ)) t || column X (-(L : ℤ) + 1) t) := by\n      simp [column, evolveFrom_succ, rule30_eq]\n    have hL : column X (-(L : ℤ)) t = decide (L % 2 = 0) := h L (by omega)\n    have hor : (column X (-(L : ℤ)) t || column X (-(L : ℤ) + 1) t) = true := by\n      cases L with\n      | zero => rw [hL]; simp\n      | succ m =>\n          have hm : column X (-(m : ℤ)) t = decide (m % 2 = 0) := h m (by omega)\n          have he : column X (-((m : ℕ) + 1 : ℤ) + 1) t = decide (m % 2 = 0) := by\n            rw [← hm]; congr 1; push_cast; ring\n          push_cast at hL he ⊢\n          rw [hL, he, alt_succ]\n          cases (decide (m % 2 = 0)) <;> simp\n    have hmax' : column X (-(L : ℤ) - 1) t ≠ !(decide (L % 2 = 0)) := by\n      have he : column X (-(L : ℤ) - 1) t = column X (-((L : ℕ) + 1 : ℤ)) t := by\n        congr 1; push_cast; ring\n      rw [he, ← alt_succ]\n      exact hmax\n    rw [hstep, hor]\n    revert hmax'\n    cases (column X (-(L : ℤ) - 1) t) <;> cases (decide (L % 2 = 0)) <;> simp",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.Rule30AlternatingStep"]","+    "id": "centerColumn_window_not_constant",","+    "lean_name": "centerColumn_window_not_constant",","+    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_window_not_constant (a : ℕ) (ha : 1 ≤ a) :\n    (∃ s ≤ 3 * a, centerColumn (a + s) = true) ∧\n      (∃ s ≤ 3 * a, centerColumn (a + s) = false) := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "The two run bounds read as one statement about a window: from time `a` to time `4 * a` the centre column is neither all black nor all white. All black would make the first `a + 1` cells of the window a black stretch, contradicting `centerColumn_black_run_lt_start` at `L = a`; all white would make all `3 * a + 1` of them a white stretch, contradicting `centerColumn_white_run_lt_start` at `L = 3 * a`. Stated with the same `3 * a` on both sides so the counting node has one window to work with rather than two. This is the interface: it is the form in which rule 30 says something about the centre column that a `Bool` sequence can fail, and the next node is the only thing that consumes it.",","+    "disclaims": "Nothing towards Prize 2: it is the two run bounds restated. The window is far too wide — measured, every window `[a, 2a]` already holds both colours — and the width is the white bound's constant rather than a fact about the picture.",","+    "witness": {","+      "expression": "(List.range 5).all fun a =>\n  decide (a < 1) ||\n    (((List.range (3 * a + 1)).any fun s => centerColumn (a + s)) &&\n      ((List.range (3 * a + 1)).any fun s => !centerColumn (a + s)))",","+      "imports": [],","+      "range": "1 ≤ a ≤ 4. `any` stops at the first witness of each colour and both are found within two cells of `a` at every `a` in range, so no read goes past depth 6."","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_long_black_runs",","-    "lean_name": "centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_long_black_runs",","-    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_long_black_runs\n    (h : ∀ k N : ℕ, ∃ t, N ≤ t ∧ ∀ s < k, centerColumn (t + s) = true) :\n    ¬ IsEventuallyPeriodic centerColumn := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "A SUFFICIENT CONDITION FOR PRIZE 1, in the shape the wall asks for. If the centre column has arbitrarily long black runs starting arbitrarily late, it is not eventually periodic. Why: a run of length `p + 1` beginning at or after the onset `N` of a period `p` is one full period of black, so periodicity copies it forward for ever and the centre column is black from that point on -- and centerColumn_not_eventually_constant (already closed) says it is white infinitely often. The work is the strong induction that carries the run forward by whole periods; everything else is arithmetic. This is the first statement on the board that attacks P1 through the centre column's RUN structure rather than through another column, and it is what centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_deep_alternating is stated on top of.",","-    "disclaims": "Does not prove Prize 1: its hypothesis is unproved and is strictly STRONGER than the conclusion, so nothing can be read back from P1 to it -- Thue-Morse is aperiodic with no run longer than two. The measured black runs of the seed's centre column grow like log2 of the depth (histogram 1996, 968, 504, 257, 120, 58, 34, 16, 4, 3 over 3,960 rows, an exact coin), so the hypothesis is very probably true and no computation can establish it.",","-    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","-    "route": {","-      "tactics": "  rintro ⟨p, hp, N, hper⟩\n  obtain ⟨t, htN, hblack⟩ := h (p + 1) N\n  have hall : ∀ s : ℕ, centerColumn (t + s) = true := by\n    intro s\n    induction s using Nat.strong_induction_on with\n    | _ s ih =>\n        by_cases hsp : s < p + 1\n        · exact hblack s hsp\n        · have key : centerColumn (t + (s - p) + p) = centerColumn (t + (s - p)) :=\n            hper (t + (s - p)) (by omega)\n          have hrw : t + (s - p) + p = t + s := by omega\n          rw [hrw] at key\n          rw [key]\n          exact ih (s - p) (by omega)\n  obtain ⟨u, huN, hu⟩ := centerColumn_not_eventually_constant.2 t\n  have hcon := hall (u - t)\n  rw [show t + (u - t) = u by omega] at hcon\n  rw [hu] at hcon\n  exact Bool.noConfusion hcon",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnNotEventuallyConstant"]","+    "id": "centerColumnCount_ge_of_pow",","+    "lean_name": "centerColumnCount_ge_of_pow",","+    "statement": "theorem centerColumnCount_ge_of_pow (n : ℕ) :\n    n ≤ ((Finset.range (5 ^ n)).filter fun t => centerColumn t = true).card ∧\n      n ≤ ((Finset.range (5 ^ n)).filter fun t => centerColumn t = false).card := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "The windows `[5 ^ k, 5 ^ (k+1))` for `k < n` are disjoint and together cover `[1, 5 ^ n)`, and each one contains the window `[5 ^ k, 4 * 5 ^ k]` that `centerColumn_window_not_constant` says holds both colours — `4 * 5 ^ k < 5 ^ (k+1)` is the whole reason the base is 5 rather than 4. So each colour picks up at least one new cell per power of 5, and by induction on `n` both counts below `5 ^ n` are at least `n`. This is the first bound on the centre column's excess that uses rule 30 at all: it gives `|2 * count N - N| ≤ N - 2 * ⌊log₅ N⌋`, where `centerColumn_density_tendsto_half_iff_excess_nat` — the ℕ form of the prize, already closed — says exactly that the same quantity is eventually below `N / d` for every `d`. So it lands in the residual's own vocabulary and in the same inequality, at a strength that is not remotely enough. The derivation was checked in Lean with the window statement taken in as a hypothesis rather than assumed available (`explorer/p2seed_scratch.lean`, builds clean), so what is proposed here is what its consumer needs and not merely something true.",","+    "disclaims": "Nothing towards Prize 2, and the gap is not a matter of sharpening. The bound is `N - 2 log₅ N`, which is `N(1 - o(1))`, where the prize needs `o(N)`; Talus's attack of 2026-09-10 shows the whole run route is capped at `N(1 - 1/(c log N))` for any run bound however sharp, so no successor of these four nodes reaches it. What it does is put one statement about `centerColumnCount` on the board that a `Bool` sequence can fail — all twelve closed P2 nodes hold of every `ℕ → Bool`, which is the measurement that named this tier.",","+    "witness": {","+      "expression": "(List.range 2).all fun n =>\n  decide (n ≤ ((Finset.range (5 ^ n)).filter fun t => centerColumn t = true).card) &&\n    decide (n ≤ ((Finset.range (5 ^ n)).filter fun t => centerColumn t = false).card)",","+      "imports": [],","+      "range": "n < 2, and I want to be plain that this is nearly vacuous: it checks the shape of the inequality and the colours are not swapped, and nothing else. `n = 2` needs `centerColumn 24`, which is depth 24 against a usable ceiling near 18, so this claim cannot carry a naive witness that reaches its content. The content was checked in the engine instead: both counts below `5 ^ n` exceed `n` by orders of magnitude at every `n ≤ 8` (`explorer/p2seed_runs.mjs` prints the same tally in base 2)."","     }","-  },","-  {","-    "id": "centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_deep_alternating",","-    "lean_name": "centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_deep_alternating",","-    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_deep_alternating\n    (h : ∀ k N : ℕ, ∃ t, N ≤ t ∧ ∀ j < k, evolve t (-(j : ℤ)) = decide (j % 2 = 0)) :\n    ¬ IsEventuallyPeriodic centerColumn := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "THE TIER'S PAYOFF: a sufficient condition for Prize 1 stated entirely about the PICTURE and not about any column. If the alternating fixed point `1 0 1 0 ...` reappears immediately left of the origin to arbitrary depth at arbitrarily late times, then the centre column is not eventually periodic. It follows in three lines from column_black_run_of_alternating (a deep alternating stretch holds the centre black that long) and centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_long_black_runs. Why it is worth having rather than being a restatement: the hypothesis mentions neither the centre column nor a period, so a proof of it need not reason about repetition at all -- it is a claim that a specific finite word of length k occurs at a specific place, infinitely often, and words-in-rows is the vocabulary of crystal 3 (every word has exactly four preimages) and of the leftward solve, neither of which any existing P1 block uses. With column_alternating_shrink it also says WHERE such an occurrence must be created: only at a white centre cell, which is the same seam obstruction 9 names.",","-    "disclaims": "DOES NOT PROVE PRIZE 1, and cannot be converted into a proof of it: the hypothesis is unproved and is strictly stronger than the conclusion (an aperiodic sequence may have bounded runs). The OR-to-XOR filter (crystals item 66) was run rather than assumed, in explorer/altrun_xor.mjs over 4,000 rows: rule 30 reaches alternating depth 9 and is still at 7 in its last quarter; rule 150 has a centre column that is black at every one of 4,000 rows and NEVER exceeds alternating depth 4; rule 90's depth is 0 in its last quarter. So the black-run-equals-alternating-depth correspondence this node rests on is false for both XOR rules -- rule 150 has unbounded runs with a bounded depth -- and the argument is not one that survives replacing OR by XOR. That is the filter passing, and it is the OR in `left XOR (centre OR right)` doing the work in two places: swallowing the right neighbour at a black origin, and turning rule 30 at position -1 into a plain negation. No naive witness for the hypothesis: it is about depths beyond any computable range, and a finite check would be `unchecked` rather than a pass.",","-    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","-    "route": {","-      "tactics": "  refine centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_long_black_runs ?_\n  intro k N\n  obtain ⟨t, htN, halt⟩ := h (k + 1) N\n  refine ⟨t, htN, ?_⟩\n  intro s hs\n  exact column_black_run_of_alternating initialConfig t k\n    (fun j hj => halt j hj) s (by omega)",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.ColumnBlackRunOfAlternating", "Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnNotIsEventuallyPeriodicOfLongBlackRuns"]","-    }","   }"," ]}"]}],"originalFile":"{"proposals": [
