    "reason": "The converse: `L + 1` consecutive black centre cells FORCE the row to be alternating `1 0 1 0 ...` for `L + 1` cells left of the origin. Why: a black centre pins the next centre cell to the negation of the cell at `-1` (column_succ_of_black), so a black run makes the cell at `-1` white; then rule 30 read at `-1`, with a white cell there and a black one at `0`, is a plain negation and hands back the cell at `-2`; and so on inward, one cell per diagonal. Together with column_black_run_of_alternating this is an exact equality, not two inequalities: the length of the centre column's black run at time `t` IS the depth to which the alternating fixed point reaches left of the origin. Verified with zero mismatches over 3,960 rows of the seed and six random rows in explorer/altrun.mjs. The work is that the induction is strong: solving for the cell at `-(j+1)` needs the cell at `-(j-1)` as well as at `-(j)`, so it recurses two steps back, not one.",
